          "grounding": "⊣=𐑥: Proline's unique cyclic side chain creates a rigid, closed-loop structure that constrains the peptide backbone into specific cyclic conformations or turns.\n⊢=𐑨: Proline imposes a rigid cyclic constraint on the peptide backbone that propagates through the extended three-dimensional spatial arrangement of the protein structure.\n⊞=𐑳: Proline is a single amino acid residue, so its participation in a peptide bond involves one molecule on each side, representing an equal 1:1 stoichiometric ratio.\n⊡=𐑴: Proline's rigid cyclic structure imposes a discrete conformational constraint (cis/trans isomerization) that acts as a Z2 topological barrier, preventing continuous deformation to the trivial state without breaking the peptide bond.\n⊙=𐑢: Proline acts as a rigid structural constraint that stabilizes the peptide backbone in a specific conformation, preventing the system from reaching a critical threshold of conformational instability or phase transition.\n⊥=𐑖: Proline's rigid pyrrolidine ring structurally enforces a fixed chirality and conformational constraint that persists through peptide bond formation, preventing thermal interconversion and providing a deep memory of its orientational state.\n≻=𐑑: Proline's unique cyclic structure imposes a rigid conformational constraint that fundamentally alters the peptide backbone geometry, representing a structural transformation distinct from standard amino acids.\n≺=𐑬: Proline's rigid cyclic structure creates a unique steric and electronic environment that enforces a specific, self-complementary geometric constraint on the peptide backbone, distinguishing it from the linear flexibility of other residues.\n⋈=𐑞: Proline's rigid cyclic structure imposes specific geometric constraints that make its participation in peptide bonds context-dependent, leading to reliable but not absolute signaling of structural turns or helix breaks.\n⊤=𐑧: Proline's rigid pyrrolidine ring imposes a high kinetic barrier to backbone rotation and conformational rearrangement, effectively freezing the local peptide structure.\n∈=𐑲: Proline's structural influence is strictly local, primarily restricting the backbone dihedral angles of the immediate peptide bond and its immediate neighbors without propagating long-range conformational changes.\n∋=𐑜: Proline is one of twenty standard amino acids, so its selection as a peptide component is disjunctive, meaning any single amino acid from the set suffices to occupy a position in the sequence.",
